{'name': 'lower_anchored_range_concepts', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "predicts like existing model 'extended_multiples_concepts' (p_yes RMSE 0.00090 < 0.002 on the 512-stimulus novelty pool) — a near-duplicate of extended_multiples_concepts, not a new hypothesis.", 'hypothesis': 'A refinement of extended_multiples_concepts: people do the same Bayesian concept learning (45 independently weighted rules plus multiples of 13-50, ranges with a preferred typical width, an inferred share of random examples, examples drawn without replacement), but they find "numbers up to N" ranges — ranges that start at 1 — a priori more (or less) natural than other ranges of the same width, because a program capped from above is a simple idea. The one change is this inferred prior boost for ranges anchored at 1, which predicts that people extend "yes" downward from the smallest example toward 1 more readily than upward beyond the largest example, the asymmetry the incumbent\'s symmetric ranges miss.', 'context': 'experiment2 round 2 candidate 3 refine incumbent extended_multiples_concepts'}

{'name': 'small_endpoint_range_prior', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "predicts like existing model 'extended_multiples_concepts' (p_yes RMSE 0.00179 < 0.002 on the 512-stimulus novelty pool) — a near-duplicate of extended_multiples_concepts, not a new hypothesis.", 'hypothesis': 'I chose log_number_line_interval_prior (pruned in experiment 1, 6.5 nats behind) and refined it. People picture ranges of numbers on a compressed mental number line, so before seeing any examples they find a range among small numbers more plausible than an equally wide range among large numbers. As a result, "yes" around examples that fit no rule reaches further below the smallest example than above the largest. The one change is the concept learner underneath. The old learner treated every range width as equally likely and drew examples with replacement. It is replaced by the current best learner (extended_multiples_concepts: 45 independently weighted rules plus a multiples-of-13-to-50 family, an inferred share of random examples, examples drawn without replacement, and a preferred typical range width). The magnitude preference, with its strength and sign inferred, now acts on top of th', 'context': 'experiment2 round 2 candidate 5 refine chosen'}

{'name': 'magnitude_biased_guessing_lapse', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "predicts like existing model 'extended_multiples_concepts' (p_yes RMSE 0.00186 < 0.002 on the 512-stimulus novelty pool) — a near-duplicate of extended_multiples_concepts, not a new hypothesis.", 'hypothesis': 'A refinement of extended_multiples_concepts: people do the same Bayesian concept learning (45 independently weighted rules plus the multiples-of-13-to-50 family, ranges with a preferred typical width, an inferred share of random examples, examples drawn without replacement), but on some share of trials they skip that inference and guess, and their guesses favour small numbers, which feel like more typical outputs of a number-producing program. The one change is this magnitude-biased guessing lapse (a response-stage lapse as in guessing_lapse_without_replacement, with a guess rate that depends on the target\'s size), which predicts that stray "yes" answers fall more on small targets below the examples than on large targets above them — the downward-over-upward extension the incumbent fails to reproduce.', 'context': 'experiment2 round 2 candidate 3 refine incumbent extended_multiples_concepts repair 1'}

{'name': 'magnitude_biased_example_sampling', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "too slow to fit: the fit of 'magnitude_biased_example_sampling' (target_accept 0.99) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.", 'hypothesis': 'A refinement of extended_multiples_concepts: people do the same Bayesian concept learning (45 independently weighted rules plus the multiples-of-13-to-50 family, ranges of 1-100 with a preferred typical width, an inferred share of random examples, examples drawn from the concept without replacement), but they do not believe the program picks every member of its concept equally often — they believe it favours larger (or, if the inferred bias runs the other way, smaller) numbers, each member\'s chance of being shown growing as a power of its magnitude. The one change is this magnitude-biased sampling of examples: because small members of a concept are then rarely shown and so cost little in the size principle, a range or rule that stretches down toward small numbers is barely penalised while one that stretches upward is, predicting that people extend "yes" below the smallest example more re', 'context': 'experiment2 round 2 candidate 4 refine incumbent extended_multiples_concepts repair 1'}

{'name': 'magnitude_biased_example_sampling', 'outcome': 'rejected', 'detail': "too slow to fit: the fit of 'magnitude_biased_example_sampling' (target_accept 0.99) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.", 'hypothesis': 'I chose the incumbent\'s learner, extended_multiples_concepts, and changed one thing: how people believe the program picks its examples from its concept. They do not assume every member is equally likely to be shown. They assume a bias by magnitude that favours the larger members (or the smaller ones, if the inferred bias is negative), with each example drawn without replacement in proportion to its weight. If people think the program favours large members, then the absence of small examples is unsurprising, so the size principle barely penalises concepts that reach well below the smallest example but fully penalises those that reach above the largest one; this predicts that "yes" extends further below the examples than above them, the asymmetry the critique reports.', 'context': 'experiment2 round 2 candidate 5 refine chosen repair 1'}

